1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,240

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:07,860
Let me introduce our
speaker for today.

3
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:09,720
We are delighted
to have Vivek, who

4
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:12,280
is a research scientist at
Google DeepMind, leading

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:15,980
research at the intersection
of AI, Science, and Medicine.

6
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:18,600
He is the lead researcher
behind Med-PaLM

7
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,200
and Med-PaLM 2, the
first AI systems

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:23,520
to obtain passing
and expert-level

9
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:28,680
scores on US medical
license exam, respectively.

10
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:32,040
Vivek also co-leads project
AMIE, a research program

11
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,560
aiming to build and democratize
medical superintelligence.

12
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:41,360
And prior to Google, he worked
on multimodal assistant systems

13
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:43,840
at Facebook AI
research, and he is also

14
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:46,840
part of the faculty for
executive education at Harvard

15
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:50,160
TH Chan School of Public Health.

16
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,140
Let's welcome him.

17
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:54,352
[APPLAUSE]

18
00:00:54,352 --> 00:00:56,160

19
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:59,460
Thank you, Karan Thank you
to the course organizers.

20
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:01,240
And thank you,
everyone, for coming in

21
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:04,760
and sorry for not
starting exactly on time.

22
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:06,860
But hopefully, this will
be a useful session.

23
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:09,505
And yeah, today I
want to be telling you

24
00:01:09,505 --> 00:01:10,880
about some of the
work that we've

25
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,360
been doing over
the past few years

26
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:18,040
at the intersection of
agentic AI systems and science

27
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:20,820
and medicine.

28
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:24,480
The underlying
theme of this work

29
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,120
is to build out generally
capable and general-purpose AI

30
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,040
systems that can act as
collaborative partners

31
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:36,680
for experts in the scientific
and medical domain,

32
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:40,960
that is, namely
scientists and doctors.

33
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:43,360
So the first project I want
to be telling you about

34
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:47,880
is this effort
called co-scientist.

35
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:52,200
So the system that we're
trying to build out over here,

36
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,200
the mission for this work
is to essentially build out

37
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,880
a collaborative
partner for scientists

38
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:02,200
and the hope is that
with this system,

39
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:04,240
we can really
accelerate the clock

40
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:07,000
speed of scientific and
biomedical discoveries

41
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,320
and really give scientists
superpowers to do

42
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:12,400
the work that they are doing.

43
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:16,360
And it's funny because
this work actually

44
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:20,000
has its genesis in
a talk that I gave

45
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,760
at Stanford a few years back.

46
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,300
So I think, as Karan
mentioned, back in 2023,

47
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:30,040
we had this paper
published in Nature

48
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:34,640
where we introduced this large
language model called Med-PaLM.

49
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:39,320
So Med-PaLM was one of the first
examples of a medically tuned

50
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,800
large language model.

51
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:45,760
So we built out the
system to do well on tasks

52
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:48,320
medical question-answering
and summarization.

53
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,520
And back then, it was
one of the first systems

54
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:53,280
to do well on this
benchmark, which

55
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:58,070
was reflective of US medical
license exam-style questions.

56
00:02:58,070 --> 00:03:02,150
So it was one of the first
models to reach passing level

57
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:04,450
and then later on a future
version of the system,

58
00:03:04,450 --> 00:03:07,550
reach like expert-level
scores on this benchmark.

59
00:03:07,550 --> 00:03:10,230
So that made headline results.

60
00:03:10,230 --> 00:03:13,770
So I think it was like October
2023 when I was at Stanford,

61
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:16,950
and I was actually giving
a talk on that system.

62
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:18,350
And at the end of
the talk, there

63
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:22,870
was this one professor
over here, Dr. Gary Peltz.

64
00:03:22,870 --> 00:03:25,950
And he kind of walked over
to me and my teammate, Tao.

65
00:03:25,950 --> 00:03:29,290
And he essentially sent us
like a follow-up email later.

66
00:03:29,290 --> 00:03:31,190
And he explained
that we are using

67
00:03:31,190 --> 00:03:33,670
this system for medical
question-answering

68
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:35,470
and summarization.

69
00:03:35,470 --> 00:03:39,190
But he also thought that you
could use large language models

70
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:41,950
for another kind of application.

71
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:47,110
So his idea was essentially
that because LLMs

72
00:03:47,110 --> 00:03:50,830
are trained on a lot of
scientific and medical tokens,

73
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:55,430
you could actually prompt it
to do hypothesis generation.

74
00:03:55,430 --> 00:03:57,070
For example, he
wanted to actually use

75
00:03:57,070 --> 00:04:02,750
it to identify causative gene
factors for rare diseases

76
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:04,910
and so on and so forth.

77
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:07,290
So it was an interesting idea.

78
00:04:07,290 --> 00:04:11,830
But back then, we were
still dealing with our PaLM

79
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:13,990
class of models over here.

80
00:04:13,990 --> 00:04:16,110
So for those of
you who don't know,

81
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:18,680
PaLM is the precursor to Gemini.

82
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:20,930
And I hope that some of you
are at least using Gemini.

83
00:04:20,930 --> 00:04:24,590
Trust me, it's good, maybe
not as good as ChatGPT or--

84
00:04:24,590 --> 00:04:29,145
I'm kidding, but you
guys should try it.

85
00:04:29,145 --> 00:04:30,770
So it was like the
precursor to Gemini.

86
00:04:30,770 --> 00:04:34,090
And Gemini itself is now
three generations old.

87
00:04:34,090 --> 00:04:36,727

88
00:04:36,727 --> 00:04:38,310
So at that point of
time, it was still

89
00:04:38,310 --> 00:04:41,852
struggling to get reliable
answers from the system.

90
00:04:41,852 --> 00:04:44,310
There would be hallucinations
all over the place and things

91
00:04:44,310 --> 00:04:45,710
like that.

92
00:04:45,710 --> 00:04:49,190
So the idea that you could
use an LLM-based system

93
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:51,990
to do something like
hypothesis generation,

94
00:04:51,990 --> 00:04:55,110
it kind of felt wild,
because hypothesis generation

95
00:04:55,110 --> 00:04:58,350
and coming up with novel,
creative scientific ideas

96
00:04:58,350 --> 00:05:00,778
is squarely in the realm
of expert scientists.

97
00:05:00,778 --> 00:05:03,070
And to think that you could
build out like an AI system

98
00:05:03,070 --> 00:05:07,470
to do that, it felt like
many, many years away.

99
00:05:07,470 --> 00:05:09,310
So I actually went
back to my team

100
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:12,350
and I asked, should we do
this project right now?

101
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:16,510
And the overwhelming
feedback was that we're

102
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:19,390
just not ready for it yet.

103
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:22,790
But sometimes, the
best projects--

104
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,870
and I think you probably
all have experienced this.

105
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:28,003
But for me, certainly,
I think some

106
00:05:28,003 --> 00:05:29,670
of the best projects
that I've worked on

107
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:32,910
are essentially examples
where we actually don't how

108
00:05:32,910 --> 00:05:35,150
to reach the end
goal, but we still

109
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:37,750
decide to just go
ahead and do this.

110
00:05:37,750 --> 00:05:41,210
So this was one of those kind
of projects where we did not

111
00:05:41,210 --> 00:05:45,470
know exactly how to clearly
approach this problem,

112
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:48,790
or what was going to be like
a solution that would lead us

113
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:50,070
to this end goal?

114
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:52,910
It's almost like you
jumping off a cliff,

115
00:05:52,910 --> 00:05:57,270
and you have to figure out
building out a flying machine

116
00:05:57,270 --> 00:05:59,350
or an airplane on the way down.

117
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:01,490
So this project is
an example of that,

118
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:03,590
where we just decide to
go ahead and do this,

119
00:06:03,590 --> 00:06:06,290
and we just try to, OK,
let's construct some evals.

120
00:06:06,290 --> 00:06:07,450
Let's make some progress.

121
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:09,790
Let's just see how
far we can push.

122
00:06:09,790 --> 00:06:12,950
And that's what
we continue to do.

123
00:06:12,950 --> 00:06:15,270
So what we initially
did was just

124
00:06:15,270 --> 00:06:17,190
using our PaLM-based system.

125
00:06:17,190 --> 00:06:19,130
We put together like
an agentic scaffold.

126
00:06:19,130 --> 00:06:20,830
And remember, this
is all like 2023.

127
00:06:20,830 --> 00:06:23,190
The word "agent"
was not being used.

128
00:06:23,190 --> 00:06:25,410
But we still put
together this scaffold,

129
00:06:25,410 --> 00:06:28,490
which had access to some
databases and some tools.

130
00:06:28,490 --> 00:06:30,530
And it could go and
retrieve that information.

131
00:06:30,530 --> 00:06:32,050
It could read that
literature up.

132
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:34,410
And then we would ask
it to predict, OK,

133
00:06:34,410 --> 00:06:38,350
what are maybe the most likely
genes to cause this rare disease

134
00:06:38,350 --> 00:06:40,230
variant, and things like that.

135
00:06:40,230 --> 00:06:41,350
And we started doing that.

136
00:06:41,350 --> 00:06:44,430
And we actually even saw
some promising results.

137
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:47,270
So there was this one
particular hypothesis

138
00:06:47,270 --> 00:06:51,670
that this system came up with
that actually Gary went on

139
00:06:51,670 --> 00:06:55,350
to validate in his lab
using these CRISPR knocking

140
00:06:55,350 --> 00:06:57,030
experiments on mouse.

141
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:58,530
And it turned out
to actually work.

142
00:06:58,530 --> 00:07:00,910
And that ended up being like
a peer-reviewed paper that

143
00:07:00,910 --> 00:07:04,230
was published in Advanced
Science a few months back.

144
00:07:04,230 --> 00:07:06,390
So that was like promising.

145
00:07:06,390 --> 00:07:09,030
But in the process of doing
that, we also realized,

146
00:07:09,030 --> 00:07:11,630
what are the limitations
of using LLMs

147
00:07:11,630 --> 00:07:15,470
in a naive manner for this
hypothesis generation task?

148
00:07:15,470 --> 00:07:18,990
And if I were to summarize
it or put it crisply,

149
00:07:18,990 --> 00:07:23,630
I would say that when you're
traditionally using LLMs, say,

150
00:07:23,630 --> 00:07:27,470
Gemini or ChatGPT or Claude, I
would argue that these models

151
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:32,570
are primarily doing
system 1-style thinking.

152
00:07:32,570 --> 00:07:35,550
So they're like coming up
with very fast responses.

153
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:39,850
So they're akin to coming up
with very intuitive responses

154
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:41,550
without deep thinking.

155
00:07:41,550 --> 00:07:45,390
So it's mostly responses with
surface-level correlations

156
00:07:45,390 --> 00:07:48,530
and pattern-matching
and things like that.

157
00:07:48,530 --> 00:07:49,760
So that's helpful.

158
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:51,260
But at the same
time, when you think

159
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:55,260
about the kind of thinking
that is needed for science

160
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:59,540
and scientific discovery, it's
actually a very different kind

161
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:00,900
of thinking.

162
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:05,700
So you would notice that it's
actually a lot more slower.

163
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:08,140
It's actually more deliberate.

164
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:09,900
It's rigorous.

165
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:12,220
And I think you all might
have experienced that,

166
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,720
but when you talk to some
of the best scientists,

167
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:18,260
they'll tell you that they've
had their best ideas when

168
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:22,280
they've been thinking about a
problem for weeks or months,

169
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:24,580
sometimes even like years.

170
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,140
So they'll tell you that, I've
always had this thing going on

171
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:28,320
in the back of my head.

172
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:29,570
I've been thinking about this.

173
00:08:29,570 --> 00:08:33,777
And suddenly, I had this nice
creative aha moment over here.

174
00:08:33,777 --> 00:08:35,860
But essentially, the point
is that that's actually

175
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:37,500
a very different
kind of thinking.

176
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:39,482
So it's more like
system 2-style thinking.

177
00:08:39,482 --> 00:08:40,940
And I think that
thinking is really

178
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:45,700
like the hallmark of science and
scientific discovery over here.

179
00:08:45,700 --> 00:08:48,460
So the key research
question for us was,

180
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:51,740
how do we build out these
AI systems that can actually

181
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:55,260
perform this structured,
rigorous scientific thinking

182
00:08:55,260 --> 00:08:57,260
that is really the
hallmark of science

183
00:08:57,260 --> 00:08:59,660
and scientific discovery?

184
00:08:59,660 --> 00:09:03,300
So that's one framing
of the problem.

185
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:05,680
Another way to
think about this is,

186
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:08,300
if you want to, say,
build out like a truly

187
00:09:08,300 --> 00:09:10,620
scientific
superintelligent system,

188
00:09:10,620 --> 00:09:12,480
how would you
progress over there?

189
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:15,140
How would you quantify progress?

190
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:18,580
So for that, I have
this graph over here.

191
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:22,540
And on the y-axis, you'll see
different scientific tasks

192
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:24,580
ordered by complexity.

193
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:26,740
So they range from things
like literature review

194
00:09:26,740 --> 00:09:30,200
and hypothesis generation
to more complex tasks

195
00:09:30,200 --> 00:09:34,700
like writing a research paper
or coming up with a PhD thesis

196
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:37,460
to something that's at
the highest levels, which

197
00:09:37,460 --> 00:09:40,140
is coming up with
paradigm shifting theories

198
00:09:40,140 --> 00:09:43,740
and breakthroughs, say, like the
general theory of relativity.

199
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:47,260
And then on the
x-axis, I have the time

200
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:50,140
it might take an
average human scientist

201
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:51,940
to perform these tasks.

202
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:54,780
So it can range from
something minutes and hours

203
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:58,780
to days, weeks, months, years,
and sometimes even decades,

204
00:09:58,780 --> 00:10:01,880
and lifetimes to really
do paradigm shifting,

205
00:10:01,880 --> 00:10:04,100
like research and theories.

206
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:08,620
And then if you were to start
asking this question, where

207
00:10:08,620 --> 00:10:11,340
do some of the popular
AI models and systems

208
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:13,200
that we have around us--

209
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:17,300
where do they actually fall
onto this graph or this plot?

210
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:21,900
So a very popular
example is AlphaFold.

211
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:24,660
So if you were to think,
where does AlphaFold

212
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:26,540
fall on this graph?

213
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:28,680
So you'd see that I
have it over here,

214
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:34,340
which is at the extreme
end of the x-axis.

215
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:38,020
So if you ask me like, why
do I have it over there,

216
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:42,320
I would argue that
AlphaFold has, in some ways,

217
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:44,660
it's already done
the work that's

218
00:10:44,660 --> 00:10:50,020
equivalent to millions of human
scientists years worth of work.

219
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:52,380
And then, if you
come and ask me why,

220
00:10:52,380 --> 00:10:55,420
you just have to think
back to the status quo

221
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:57,380
before AlphaFold existed.

222
00:10:57,380 --> 00:10:59,980
So before AlphaFold
existed, to get

223
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:02,140
to the structure of
a single protein,

224
00:11:02,140 --> 00:11:06,520
that was often an entire PhD's
worth of effort over here.

225
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:09,580
So it would almost take three to
five years of dedicated PhD work

226
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:12,860
to get to the structure
of a single protein.

227
00:11:12,860 --> 00:11:15,780
And then literally once
AlphaFold, the model

228
00:11:15,780 --> 00:11:19,660
was developed and the system
came online, literally

229
00:11:19,660 --> 00:11:23,680
overnight, we have predictions
for millions of protein

230
00:11:23,680 --> 00:11:24,740
literally overnight.

231
00:11:24,740 --> 00:11:26,440
So if you were to
think about that,

232
00:11:26,440 --> 00:11:28,980
it's done the work
equivalent to millions

233
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:31,660
of years of human
scientists worth of time

234
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:33,420
and effort over here.

235
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:35,440
So that's very cool, obviously.

236
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:39,380
And it was recognized with the
Nobel Prize for that reason.

237
00:11:39,380 --> 00:11:42,900
But at the same time, if
you look at this graph,

238
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:45,140
you'll actually notice that
I actually have AlphaFold

239
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:47,140
outside of this graph.

240
00:11:47,140 --> 00:11:50,300
And the reason for
that is, at least when

241
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:54,220
I think about a scientific
superintelligent system,

242
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:56,620
I actually think about
generality being a very

243
00:11:56,620 --> 00:11:58,940
important property over here.

244
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:01,680
And again, if you
were to ask me,

245
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:05,260
I would say that the one thing
that AlphaFold doesn't have

246
00:12:05,260 --> 00:12:07,380
is this property of generality.

247
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:09,220
So it's like a very
specialized model.

248
00:12:09,220 --> 00:12:11,660
So the only inputs
that it recognizes

249
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:15,900
are protein sequences, and the
only outputs that it can produce

