00:24:07,990 --> 00:24:10,990
might tell you, go and explore
this initial direction over

496
00:24:10,990 --> 00:24:15,010
here, the scientist can say,
OK, this is the problem,

497
00:24:15,010 --> 00:24:17,827
but I think the solution
might lie over here.

498
00:24:17,827 --> 00:24:19,410
So go and figure
this out essentially.

499
00:24:19,410 --> 00:24:22,230
So they can give the system
initial like seed directions

500
00:24:22,230 --> 00:24:23,870
to explore over here.

501
00:24:23,870 --> 00:24:26,910
And again, they can also
give additional form

502
00:24:26,910 --> 00:24:28,490
of multimodal data over here.

503
00:24:28,490 --> 00:24:31,950
So it can be PDFs of
related publications

504
00:24:31,950 --> 00:24:33,470
and things like that.

505
00:24:33,470 --> 00:24:36,390
It can also be additional
experimental data over here

506
00:24:36,390 --> 00:24:38,480
that you might have
generated in the lab.

507
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:40,230
So essentially, that
all forms the context

508
00:24:40,230 --> 00:24:42,630
that is needed for
the system to perform

509
00:24:42,630 --> 00:24:45,473
its hypothesis generation task.

510
00:24:45,473 --> 00:24:47,890
So that's all structured and
set up as this research goal.

511
00:24:47,890 --> 00:24:50,230
And that's the
input to the system.

512
00:24:50,230 --> 00:24:52,910
And then what the system
does is it kind of does

513
00:24:52,910 --> 00:24:55,330
a bunch of computation.

514
00:24:55,330 --> 00:24:57,030
And as I said, this is dynamic.

515
00:24:57,030 --> 00:25:00,050
So it can go on for as
much time as you want.

516
00:25:00,050 --> 00:25:03,390
So it can go on for say a
few minutes, to a few hours,

517
00:25:03,390 --> 00:25:05,450
to sometimes even
days and weeks,

518
00:25:05,450 --> 00:25:08,350
depending on the complexity
of the problem here.

519
00:25:08,350 --> 00:25:11,390
And at the end of that what the
system produces is essentially,

520
00:25:11,390 --> 00:25:14,750
I would say, a research
report with a bunch

521
00:25:14,750 --> 00:25:18,070
of hypotheses or
solutions towards solving

522
00:25:18,070 --> 00:25:21,110
the problem that the scientist
had articulated over here.

523
00:25:21,110 --> 00:25:24,010
So again, the interface
is very simple.

524
00:25:24,010 --> 00:25:29,070
It's a research goal in and
research proposal or research

525
00:25:29,070 --> 00:25:31,550
summary out with a bunch
of hypotheses or solutions

526
00:25:31,550 --> 00:25:34,270
to solve the problem.

527
00:25:34,270 --> 00:25:36,730
And then within the
system, as I said,

528
00:25:36,730 --> 00:25:38,710
it's like a multiagent system.

529
00:25:38,710 --> 00:25:40,390
And the way these
agents are configured

530
00:25:40,390 --> 00:25:42,990
are essentially to
continuously generate

531
00:25:42,990 --> 00:25:45,050
different kinds of
scientific hypotheses,

532
00:25:45,050 --> 00:25:48,430
but also review, critique,
rank, and prioritize them

533
00:25:48,430 --> 00:25:50,710
over a period of time.

534
00:25:50,710 --> 00:25:54,710
And these agents
are all essentially

535
00:25:54,710 --> 00:25:57,630
our latest and greatest like
Gemini models over here.

536
00:25:57,630 --> 00:26:01,470
And we set them up with
different kinds of strategies

537
00:26:01,470 --> 00:26:02,990
and system prompts.

538
00:26:02,990 --> 00:26:08,070
So we force them to do some
very specific specialized tasks

539
00:26:08,070 --> 00:26:10,950
within this multiagent setup.

540
00:26:10,950 --> 00:26:15,030
So again, there's
a bunch of texts,

541
00:26:15,030 --> 00:26:16,470
but I would say
that the simplest

542
00:26:16,470 --> 00:26:18,750
way to think about this
system is to essentially

543
00:26:18,750 --> 00:26:22,070
think about it a computer
program with a while loop.

544
00:26:22,070 --> 00:26:25,190
So there's one
function or one method

545
00:26:25,190 --> 00:26:27,630
which corresponds to
continuously generating

546
00:26:27,630 --> 00:26:30,350
different scientific
ideas and hypotheses.

547
00:26:30,350 --> 00:26:33,190
There's another function
to review and critique

548
00:26:33,190 --> 00:26:35,230
those hypotheses over here.

549
00:26:35,230 --> 00:26:39,293
There's a third function to rank
and prioritize these hypotheses.

550
00:26:39,293 --> 00:26:40,710
And then there's
a fourth function

551
00:26:40,710 --> 00:26:43,970
over here, which is to improve
and evolve those hypotheses.

552
00:26:43,970 --> 00:26:46,790
So it's like a while loop
with these four functions

553
00:26:46,790 --> 00:26:50,110
that are kind of running
asynchronously and continually.

554
00:26:50,110 --> 00:26:54,390
And all these functions are
implemented using agentic LLM

555
00:26:54,390 --> 00:26:57,910
approaches over here.

556
00:26:57,910 --> 00:27:00,750
So as I mentioned,
each of these agents

557
00:27:00,750 --> 00:27:10,110
have their own system
prompt set up over here.

558
00:27:10,110 --> 00:27:12,790
So we call this a
library of strategies.

559
00:27:12,790 --> 00:27:17,150
So we give each
specific agent a set

560
00:27:17,150 --> 00:27:20,830
of strategies that can select
from to perform like the tasks

561
00:27:20,830 --> 00:27:22,270
that it has been given.

562
00:27:22,270 --> 00:27:25,170
So, for example, to
generate scientific ideas,

563
00:27:25,170 --> 00:27:28,550
one strategy is to go
read all related research

564
00:27:28,550 --> 00:27:30,390
papers to that
given goal, and then

565
00:27:30,390 --> 00:27:32,630
use those research
papers to build up

566
00:27:32,630 --> 00:27:35,450
a foundation of knowledge,
and then use that to propose,

567
00:27:35,450 --> 00:27:38,190
some idea over here.

568
00:27:38,190 --> 00:27:40,750
Another strategy to come
up with scientific ideas

569
00:27:40,750 --> 00:27:42,830
is to simulate this debate.

570
00:27:42,830 --> 00:27:45,670
So I think some of you
might have also experienced

571
00:27:45,670 --> 00:27:47,590
this where sometimes
you get your best

572
00:27:47,590 --> 00:27:48,970
ideas by talking to a friend.

573
00:27:48,970 --> 00:27:51,810
So you both start off
with some initial ideas,

574
00:27:51,810 --> 00:27:53,830
but then you have a back
and forth conversation.

575
00:27:53,830 --> 00:27:56,430
And then what
emerges out of that

576
00:27:56,430 --> 00:27:59,550
is an idea that is much better
than what you had both initially

577
00:27:59,550 --> 00:28:01,630
to begin with.

578
00:28:01,630 --> 00:28:04,210
So we try to simulate that
within an agentic setup

579
00:28:04,210 --> 00:28:04,710
over here.

580
00:28:04,710 --> 00:28:08,190
We asked the agent to simulate
an interaction between two

581
00:28:08,190 --> 00:28:08,990
experts.

582
00:28:08,990 --> 00:28:11,172
Let it go on for a few
turns of conversation.

583
00:28:11,172 --> 00:28:12,630
And then at the
end of that come up

584
00:28:12,630 --> 00:28:15,830
with a scientific idea or
a hypothesis over here.

585
00:28:15,830 --> 00:28:19,830
So essentially, there's hundreds
of difference of strategies

586
00:28:19,830 --> 00:28:21,330
over here, like
within each agent,

587
00:28:21,330 --> 00:28:23,300
including the [INAUDIBLE] agent.

588
00:28:23,300 --> 00:28:27,860
And in fact, one of the favorite
pastimes of all our team

589
00:28:27,860 --> 00:28:31,140
members is essentially that
they would spend time listening

590
00:28:31,140 --> 00:28:33,780
to these podcasts from,
say, Terence Tao or David

591
00:28:33,780 --> 00:28:35,140
Deutsch and others.

592
00:28:35,140 --> 00:28:37,940
And they would see,
how are these people

593
00:28:37,940 --> 00:28:39,360
thinking in an abstract manner?

594
00:28:39,360 --> 00:28:41,720
How are they coming up
with new scientific ideas?

595
00:28:41,720 --> 00:28:44,060
And then they would try
to abstract and summarize

596
00:28:44,060 --> 00:28:47,180
them and implement them
as a prompt within this

597
00:28:47,180 --> 00:28:49,300
library of strategies
that we have.

598
00:28:49,300 --> 00:28:52,820
And it's a continual evolving
body of knowledge over here,

599
00:28:52,820 --> 00:28:54,560
so you can keep on adding it.

600
00:28:54,560 --> 00:28:58,180
And then at test time, you just
have to sample one strategy

601
00:28:58,180 --> 00:29:03,058
and then use that to generate a
scientific hypothesis over here.

602
00:29:03,058 --> 00:29:05,100
So similarly, we have a
bunch of these strategies

603
00:29:05,100 --> 00:29:08,380
for the reflection agent,
which is essentially

604
00:29:08,380 --> 00:29:10,180
doing like this review.

605
00:29:10,180 --> 00:29:12,500
And then the agent that
I want to maybe spend

606
00:29:12,500 --> 00:29:18,420
more time on over here is
actually this ranking agent.

607
00:29:18,420 --> 00:29:21,500
So what this agent does
is it kind of takes

608
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,980
the hypotheses and the ideas
that have been generated so far.

609
00:29:24,980 --> 00:29:29,020
And then it kind of tries
to rank and prioritize them.

610
00:29:29,020 --> 00:29:31,740
And the way it does that is
using this debate mechanism

611
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:34,180
that I mentioned before.

612
00:29:34,180 --> 00:29:37,980
So what it does is it
orchestrates these debate

613
00:29:37,980 --> 00:29:40,380
matches between these ideas.

614
00:29:40,380 --> 00:29:44,560
And then it tries to simulate
a few turns of conversation.

615
00:29:44,560 --> 00:29:48,340
And then essentially, the
goal is to rank these ideas

616
00:29:48,340 --> 00:29:52,540
and say, which one is
better based on the criteria

617
00:29:52,540 --> 00:29:54,860
that the scientist had
articulated initially

618
00:29:54,860 --> 00:29:56,700
in their research goal?

619
00:29:56,700 --> 00:29:58,020
So that's the setup.

620
00:29:58,020 --> 00:30:01,080
So it does these
scientific debates.

621
00:30:01,080 --> 00:30:04,700
And once you have a few of these
debates set up and run, what

622
00:30:04,700 --> 00:30:07,300
you can then start doing
is you can actually

623
00:30:07,300 --> 00:30:09,420
start like computing
these Elo scores.

624
00:30:09,420 --> 00:30:12,000
So much like in a Go or
in a chess tournament,

625
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:16,560
you can use the Elo ratings to
and rank players, similarly,

626
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:18,820
you can take the same
approach over here

627
00:30:18,820 --> 00:30:20,980
and use that to
rank and prioritize

628
00:30:20,980 --> 00:30:23,700
the different scientific
hypotheses over here.

629
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:29,460
And what purpose it serves
is essentially this.

630
00:30:29,460 --> 00:30:34,020
So if you were to simply have
only a generation or a review

631
00:30:34,020 --> 00:30:36,420
agent within the
system, then I think

632
00:30:36,420 --> 00:30:39,940
what that would end up
being is a system that's

633
00:30:39,940 --> 00:30:45,310
generating many, many like good
ideas or decent ideas over here.

634
00:30:45,310 --> 00:30:47,060
But I would argue that
that's actually not

635
00:30:47,060 --> 00:30:50,500
enough to move the needle in
terms of scientific discovery

636
00:30:50,500 --> 00:30:51,380
over here.

637
00:30:51,380 --> 00:30:54,942
Because when you're
talking to scientists,

638
00:30:54,942 --> 00:30:56,400
you would see that
they're actually

639
00:30:56,400 --> 00:30:58,360
not short on ideas over here.

640
00:30:58,360 --> 00:31:01,380
The expert scientists
have more ideas

641
00:31:01,380 --> 00:31:04,140
than they have time
and resources for.

642
00:31:04,140 --> 00:31:05,940
So if you like
truly building out

643
00:31:05,940 --> 00:31:08,000
a collaborative partner
for these scientists,

644
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:11,740
then you have to respect their
time and their attention,

645
00:31:11,740 --> 00:31:14,820
and only surface ideas that
are really worth their time

646
00:31:14,820 --> 00:31:16,080
and their attention over here.

647
00:31:16,080 --> 00:31:19,020
So you have to do a very good
job of prioritizing the outputs

648
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:21,780
from the system, really
summarizing them,

649
00:31:21,780 --> 00:31:25,140
and also have this notion
of epistemic humility

650
00:31:25,140 --> 00:31:26,740
within the system.

651
00:31:26,740 --> 00:31:29,260
And when I say epistemic
humility, what I mean

652
00:31:29,260 --> 00:31:32,460
is that the system should
be able to really convey

653
00:31:32,460 --> 00:31:34,940
when it actually
doesn't about something.

654
00:31:34,940 --> 00:31:36,740
So it should be able
to clearly articulate

655
00:31:36,740 --> 00:31:39,380
what is its confidence
about a given hypothesis

656
00:31:39,380 --> 00:31:41,700
and also say, what are
the key uncertainties that

657
00:31:41,700 --> 00:31:44,020
need resolving over here?

658
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:47,320
So that is what this
ranking agent tries to do.

659
00:31:47,320 --> 00:31:50,000
So it essentially tries
to prioritize these ideas.

660
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:53,940
It tries to say, which one is
better and which one's spend--

661
00:31:53,940 --> 00:31:56,860
is worth spending like
time and attention on.

662
00:31:56,860 --> 00:31:59,100
And it also tries
to compute, what

663
00:31:59,100 --> 00:32:02,500
is the uncertainty associated
with each idea over here,

664
00:32:02,500 --> 00:32:05,340
and propagate that
to the scientist.

665
00:32:05,340 --> 00:32:07,602
So that's one purpose
that it serves.

666
00:32:07,602 --> 00:32:09,060
And then the second
purpose that it

667
00:32:09,060 --> 00:32:12,940
serves is essentially, because
these debates are all happening

668
00:32:12,940 --> 00:32:15,980
in natural language,
what you can do

669
00:32:15,980 --> 00:32:19,140
is you can create
summaries of them.

670
00:32:19,140 --> 00:32:21,140
And then you can put
all of those summaries

671
00:32:21,140 --> 00:32:25,060
back into the overall
memory of the system.

672
00:32:25,060 --> 00:32:29,140
And then what happens is the
next time any of these agents

673
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:33,920
are doing their work, what
they can do is they can use--

674
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:36,020
read this summary
from the memory

675
00:32:36,020 --> 00:32:38,940
and then use that to, say,
come up with better hypotheses

676
00:32:38,940 --> 00:32:42,780
or do a better job at generating
reviews, and so on and so forth.

677
00:32:42,780 --> 00:32:45,100
And what that helps is it
creates this nice feedback

678
00:32:45,100 --> 00:32:48,820
loop in the system that leads
to a self-improving cycle

679
00:32:48,820 --> 00:32:50,580
over here.

680
00:32:50,580 --> 00:32:53,000
So that's the crux
of the computation.

681
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:54,400
So as I said, it's quite simple.

682
00:32:54,400 --> 00:32:57,580
It's a while loop with
four methods over here.

683
00:32:57,580 --> 00:33:00,280
And if you were to zoom
out and think about it,

684
00:33:00,280 --> 00:33:04,820
it's almost like, I would
say, an agentic in silico

685
00:33:04,820 --> 00:33:07,380
implementation of the
thought process that

686
00:33:07,380 --> 00:33:09,340
happens in a scientist's
head when they're

687
00:33:09,340 --> 00:33:11,940
trying to come up with
say new scientific ideas

688
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:14,180
and things like that.

689
00:33:14,180 --> 00:33:17,460
And then once you let this
computation go on for a set

690
00:33:17,460 --> 00:33:19,580
period of time, and
that depends on when

691
00:33:19,580 --> 00:33:20,947
you reach your end state--

692
00:33:20,947 --> 00:33:22,780
and again, there's a
few mechanisms in which

693
00:33:22,780 --> 00:33:24,000
you can reach this end state.

694
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:26,340
So one way could
be like, you say

695
00:33:26,340 --> 00:33:29,420
keep on going until you
have x number of ideas

696
00:33:29,420 --> 00:33:32,300
or another method could be like
you could just tell the system,

697
00:33:32,300 --> 00:33:37,300
maybe just stop when you can
no longer solve the problem

698
00:33:37,300 --> 00:33:39,740
or you're stuck over here.

699
00:33:39,740 --> 00:33:43,140
So once the system reaches
that end state, what it does

700
00:33:43,140 --> 00:33:46,540
is it visually clusters all
the ideas that it has explored

701
00:33:46,540 --> 00:33:50,180
so far and then puts all of them
together into this nice summary

702
00:33:50,180 --> 00:33:54,658
doc, which in turn is then fed
back to the scientist over here.

703
00:33:54,658 --> 00:33:55,200
So that's it.

704
00:33:55,200 --> 00:33:56,760
So that's the crux
of the system.

705
00:33:56,760 --> 00:33:58,460
And as I said,
again, all of these

706
00:33:58,460 --> 00:34:02,420
are agents implemented using
our latest and greatest Gemini

707
00:34:02,420 --> 00:34:04,660
models, so they have
all the long context

708
00:34:04,660 --> 00:34:08,780
capabilities, the multimodal
capabilities, the agentic tool

709
00:34:08,780 --> 00:34:13,100
use capabilities, and everything
that we need over here.

710
00:34:13,100 --> 00:34:15,110
Yeah, so hopefully,
this was helpful,

711
00:34:15,110 --> 00:34:16,810
but maybe I'll stop
over here right now

712
00:34:16,810 --> 00:34:18,570
and maybe take a few
questions, because I

713
00:34:18,570 --> 00:34:21,370
thought that was quite meaty.

714
00:34:21,370 --> 00:34:22,210
Anyone?

715
00:34:22,210 --> 00:34:23,489
Yes.

716
00:34:23,489 --> 00:34:25,889
How is the reward systems Go?

717
00:34:25,889 --> 00:34:31,370
How would you evaluate whether
a hypothesis is good or not?

718
00:34:31,370 --> 00:34:35,170
Even for scientists, different
people have different views.

719
00:34:35,170 --> 00:34:37,698
How this agent system
could be evaluate

720
00:34:37,698 --> 00:34:40,130
what's better [INAUDIBLE].

721
00:34:40,130 --> 00:34:42,449
So that's the next
half of the talk.

722
00:34:42,449 --> 00:34:44,730
But-- yeah.

723
00:34:44,730 --> 00:34:50,170
25 different, but the only
co-scientist [INAUDIBLE]

724
00:34:50,170 --> 00:34:52,370
in the 25 preprint--

725
00:34:52,370 --> 00:34:55,290
co-scientist [INAUDIBLE]
system didn't really

726
00:34:55,290 --> 00:34:57,810
see much performance,
saturation, or plateauing

727
00:34:57,810 --> 00:34:59,110
with test time compute.

728
00:34:59,110 --> 00:35:02,730
Have you explored the scaling
of that further [INAUDIBLE]?

729
00:35:02,730 --> 00:35:04,170
Yeah.

730
00:35:04,170 --> 00:35:06,450
So yeah, we can--

731
00:35:06,450 --> 00:35:07,130
sorry.

732
00:35:07,130 --> 00:35:10,630
We can discuss that
in more detail.

733
00:35:10,630 --> 00:35:14,210
So I think it comes down
to the class of problems

734
00:35:14,210 --> 00:35:16,250
that you're working with.

735
00:35:16,250 --> 00:35:19,290
So there's a class of problems
where the search space is

736
00:35:19,290 --> 00:35:23,210
so big that if you
keep on throwing more

737
00:35:23,210 --> 00:35:26,270
compute at the problem and
let the problem intelligently

738
00:35:26,270 --> 00:35:29,250
explore the search space, then
it is very likely to come up

739
00:35:29,250 --> 00:35:31,610
with better solutions.

740
00:35:31,610 --> 00:35:35,090
So for those classes of
problems, essentially,

741
00:35:35,090 --> 00:35:37,890
there's in some ways no limits.

742
00:35:37,890 --> 00:35:39,770
So these classes of
problems are where you're

743
00:35:39,770 --> 00:35:41,330
trying to optimize something.

