989
00:47:14,847 --> 00:47:17,430
And that's the reason why these
physician-scientists picked up

990
00:47:17,430 --> 00:47:20,750
and ran these experiments.

991
00:47:20,750 --> 00:47:25,030
So this one has the results from
the liver fibrosis experiment

992
00:47:25,030 --> 00:47:26,590
that I mentioned.

993
00:47:26,590 --> 00:47:30,870
So over here Dr. Peltz, he
took like four suggested drugs

994
00:47:30,870 --> 00:47:34,630
from the system,
and he tested it

995
00:47:34,630 --> 00:47:37,250
in his hepatic human
organoid setup.

996
00:47:37,250 --> 00:47:39,310
So these are liver organoids.

997
00:47:39,310 --> 00:47:43,750
And essentially, all the four
drugs over here, all of them

998
00:47:43,750 --> 00:47:47,630
show very promising
antifibrotic activity.

999
00:47:47,630 --> 00:47:49,710
And in fact, this one,
this suggested one

1000
00:47:49,710 --> 00:47:53,270
over here, this is actually
a well-known and safe

1001
00:47:53,270 --> 00:47:56,250
and FDA-approved
anticancer drug over here.

1002
00:47:56,250 --> 00:47:57,910
It's called vorinostat.

1003
00:47:57,910 --> 00:48:00,030
And the reason
this is interesting

1004
00:48:00,030 --> 00:48:03,110
is because it kind shows
the complementarity

1005
00:48:03,110 --> 00:48:06,710
of the human intelligence
and the AI over here.

1006
00:48:06,710 --> 00:48:09,710
So if you're a liver
fibrosis expert,

1007
00:48:09,710 --> 00:48:12,830
you may not be aware what's
happening in the cancer space.

1008
00:48:12,830 --> 00:48:15,090
So you may not be thinking
about all the therapies,

1009
00:48:15,090 --> 00:48:16,507
all the progress,
all the pathways

1010
00:48:16,507 --> 00:48:18,110
that people are researching.

1011
00:48:18,110 --> 00:48:21,630
Whereas this AI is kind of able
to go broad, and see what's

1012
00:48:21,630 --> 00:48:24,670
happening over there, and make
these unexpected connections,

1013
00:48:24,670 --> 00:48:27,390
and present that diverse
human viewpoint back

1014
00:48:27,390 --> 00:48:30,390
to the human scientists who
can then use their own judgment

1015
00:48:30,390 --> 00:48:32,110
and their deep
expertise to figure out,

1016
00:48:32,110 --> 00:48:33,250
does it make sense or not?

1017
00:48:33,250 --> 00:48:36,050
So in some ways, humans
have this deep expertise,

1018
00:48:36,050 --> 00:48:37,830
whereas this AI can go broad.

1019
00:48:37,830 --> 00:48:40,350
And if you're able to
intelligently bring these two

1020
00:48:40,350 --> 00:48:42,630
kinds of intelligence
together, then hopefully what

1021
00:48:42,630 --> 00:48:44,690
you would get, the
sum of the parts,

1022
00:48:44,690 --> 00:48:47,870
hopefully, that's much greater
than the individual systems

1023
00:48:47,870 --> 00:48:49,030
alone.

1024
00:48:49,030 --> 00:48:52,230
So this is a good
example of that.

1025
00:48:52,230 --> 00:48:55,220
So this is another
more interesting

1026
00:48:55,220 --> 00:48:57,100
a recent one over here.

1027
00:48:57,100 --> 00:49:00,220
And over here, what
happened is, there

1028
00:49:00,220 --> 00:49:06,060
were a few researchers based out
of the Sainsbury Lab in the UK.

1029
00:49:06,060 --> 00:49:10,260
So they actually had a bunch
of these AlphaFold structures

1030
00:49:10,260 --> 00:49:11,540
over here.

1031
00:49:11,540 --> 00:49:13,920
And they were trying
to figure out,

1032
00:49:13,920 --> 00:49:15,640
how do I actually analyze this?

1033
00:49:15,640 --> 00:49:18,710
How do I discover
anomalies in this data,

1034
00:49:18,710 --> 00:49:22,980
like which structures are the
ones that are worth paying

1035
00:49:22,980 --> 00:49:24,700
attention to over here?

1036
00:49:24,700 --> 00:49:27,100
So what they asked is--
they used the co-scientist

1037
00:49:27,100 --> 00:49:31,280
to build up this structural
novelty index over here.

1038
00:49:31,280 --> 00:49:34,060
So it's essentially a
bunch of these features.

1039
00:49:34,060 --> 00:49:35,540
And then they used
these features

1040
00:49:35,540 --> 00:49:38,980
to reanalyze the AlphaFold
structural predictions

1041
00:49:38,980 --> 00:49:40,340
and the data.

1042
00:49:40,340 --> 00:49:42,500
And what they were
able to uncover

1043
00:49:42,500 --> 00:49:46,180
was actually a completely
novel and previously

1044
00:49:46,180 --> 00:49:50,980
unknown a massive potato
immunoprotein over here.

1045
00:49:50,980 --> 00:49:53,740
So usually, what
plant biologists

1046
00:49:53,740 --> 00:49:56,583
assumed was that
most of these plant

1047
00:49:56,583 --> 00:49:58,500
immunoproteins-- they're
called resistasomes--

1048
00:49:58,500 --> 00:50:02,060
they'll have this
hexagonal shape,

1049
00:50:02,060 --> 00:50:06,520
whereas what this new
insight or this new analysis,

1050
00:50:06,520 --> 00:50:09,260
what it uncovered, was they're
actually much more novel

1051
00:50:09,260 --> 00:50:11,400
and massive immunoproteins.

1052
00:50:11,400 --> 00:50:12,880
So it's called 11-mer over here.

1053
00:50:12,880 --> 00:50:16,700
Essentially, that's
quite wild in some sense.

1054
00:50:16,700 --> 00:50:20,280
So this was, again, a preprint
that came out a few weeks back.

1055
00:50:20,280 --> 00:50:23,540
And I think this has quite
massive implications in terms

1056
00:50:23,540 --> 00:50:26,860
of decoding plant
immunity and how

1057
00:50:26,860 --> 00:50:30,300
that might impact agriculture
and global food security

1058
00:50:30,300 --> 00:50:33,340
and so on and so forth.

1059
00:50:33,340 --> 00:50:34,620
This is another example.

1060
00:50:34,620 --> 00:50:37,780
So this is where we
have co-scientist

1061
00:50:37,780 --> 00:50:39,402
using AlphaFold in the loop.

1062
00:50:39,402 --> 00:50:40,860
And what we have
it doing over here

1063
00:50:40,860 --> 00:50:44,620
is to design these
better Oct4 proteins.

1064
00:50:44,620 --> 00:50:47,780
So Oct4 is one of those
classic Yamanaka factors.

1065
00:50:47,780 --> 00:50:51,900
So these were the set of
transcription factors for which

1066
00:50:51,900 --> 00:50:54,500
Professor Yamanaka was
awarded the Nobel Prize back

1067
00:50:54,500 --> 00:50:57,140
in, I think, 2010
or 2011 over here.

1068
00:50:57,140 --> 00:50:59,340
But the thing with these
transcription factors

1069
00:50:59,340 --> 00:51:02,640
is, while they
rejuvenate the cell,

1070
00:51:02,640 --> 00:51:06,960
they can also cause tumors
and things like that.

1071
00:51:06,960 --> 00:51:10,300
So you want to have better
versions of these proteins that

1072
00:51:10,300 --> 00:51:14,020
have the same activity but
are more safe over here.

1073
00:51:14,020 --> 00:51:16,560
So that's what we
ask the system to do.

1074
00:51:16,560 --> 00:51:18,380
So we asked it to
predict and design

1075
00:51:18,380 --> 00:51:21,140
better sequences that
have the right activity

1076
00:51:21,140 --> 00:51:22,980
and the right
delivery properties

1077
00:51:22,980 --> 00:51:25,260
and so on and so forth.

1078
00:51:25,260 --> 00:51:28,080
So we asked it to use
AlphaFold in a loop.

1079
00:51:28,080 --> 00:51:29,140
And that's what it does.

1080
00:51:29,140 --> 00:51:31,960
So it makes a prediction
of a sequence.

1081
00:51:31,960 --> 00:51:35,460
It uses AlphaFold to look
at the structural stability

1082
00:51:35,460 --> 00:51:37,840
and then uses that to
refine it hypotheses.

1083
00:51:37,840 --> 00:51:40,580
And it does this in
silico a bunch of times.

1084
00:51:40,580 --> 00:51:42,580
And then what we observe
is the final sequence

1085
00:51:42,580 --> 00:51:45,005
that emerges that actually
has the right kind of activity

1086
00:51:45,005 --> 00:51:47,380
that we're looking for over
here and the right properties

1087
00:51:47,380 --> 00:51:49,175
that we're looking
for over here.

1088
00:51:49,175 --> 00:51:50,800
So we're doing a lot
of this right now.

1089
00:51:50,800 --> 00:51:52,883
We're taking and testing
all these de novo protein

1090
00:51:52,883 --> 00:51:54,820
designs in the lab,
and they're all looking

1091
00:51:54,820 --> 00:51:58,940
extremely promising right now.

1092
00:51:58,940 --> 00:52:00,480
So this is another example.

1093
00:52:00,480 --> 00:52:03,060
So this one's unpublished yet.

1094
00:52:03,060 --> 00:52:05,300
But over here, what we
ask the system to do

1095
00:52:05,300 --> 00:52:07,400
was to go read all
the literature,

1096
00:52:07,400 --> 00:52:12,600
look at all the data, and
then predict these new, say,

1097
00:52:12,600 --> 00:52:15,620
genetic factors or these
secreted proteins that

1098
00:52:15,620 --> 00:52:20,260
might actually take a cell
and rejuvenate it or reduce

1099
00:52:20,260 --> 00:52:22,800
the number of senescence
cells over here.

1100
00:52:22,800 --> 00:52:25,260
So senescence essentially
means your cell essentially

1101
00:52:25,260 --> 00:52:28,860
becomes so inflamed that
it doesn't divide anymore,

1102
00:52:28,860 --> 00:52:30,440
and it causes
disease essentially.

1103
00:52:30,440 --> 00:52:32,940
So you want to reduce the number
of such cells in your body

1104
00:52:32,940 --> 00:52:35,940
and then hopefully you can
reverse them back and make

1105
00:52:35,940 --> 00:52:38,420
them like younger and
be more reflective

1106
00:52:38,420 --> 00:52:40,595
of younger
individuals over here.

1107
00:52:40,595 --> 00:52:42,220
So that was what the
task that we gave.

1108
00:52:42,220 --> 00:52:44,900
So we told the system,
go find these novel

1109
00:52:44,900 --> 00:52:48,720
or unstudied genetic
factors and things like that

1110
00:52:48,720 --> 00:52:50,500
and just give us a few.

1111
00:52:50,500 --> 00:52:51,720
So that's what it did.

1112
00:52:51,720 --> 00:52:52,920
So it ran for a few days.

1113
00:52:52,920 --> 00:52:54,495
It gave us a list.

1114
00:52:54,495 --> 00:52:56,620
So we tested like a few of
them, and we compared it

1115
00:52:56,620 --> 00:52:59,220
with this positive
control, like Klotho,

1116
00:52:59,220 --> 00:53:01,020
which is very well studied.

1117
00:53:01,020 --> 00:53:04,620
And essentially, we see that
the AI-nominated factors,

1118
00:53:04,620 --> 00:53:07,260
they actually have the same
fold reduction in terms

1119
00:53:07,260 --> 00:53:11,700
of the number of percentage
of senescent cells over here

1120
00:53:11,700 --> 00:53:14,220
compared to this
positive control.

1121
00:53:14,220 --> 00:53:16,900
So essentially, if
there's a plays out--

1122
00:53:16,900 --> 00:53:19,820
we have to do more
experiments over here--

1123
00:53:19,820 --> 00:53:22,720
we might have some net
new rejuvenation factors.

1124
00:53:22,720 --> 00:53:24,700
And that would, again,
be extremely cool

1125
00:53:24,700 --> 00:53:26,280
if there's-- works out.

1126
00:53:26,280 --> 00:53:29,340

1127
00:53:29,340 --> 00:53:31,740
Yeah, and then maybe
another example

1128
00:53:31,740 --> 00:53:33,500
that I would want
to very quickly walk

1129
00:53:33,500 --> 00:53:39,060
you through is a case study
related to Alzheimer's disease.

1130
00:53:39,060 --> 00:53:43,140
So this is similar to the
antimicrobial resistance example

1131
00:53:43,140 --> 00:53:46,900
that I showed before, which
was also a recapitulation.

1132
00:53:46,900 --> 00:53:49,610
But over here, it's a bit
more complex in the sense

1133
00:53:49,610 --> 00:53:53,130
that there were certain
researchers at Mass General

1134
00:53:53,130 --> 00:53:54,352
Hospital.

1135
00:53:54,352 --> 00:53:55,810
And what they were
trying to do was

1136
00:53:55,810 --> 00:54:02,150
they were trying to study this
linkage between ACE inhibitors,

1137
00:54:02,150 --> 00:54:04,130
which are, again,
very commonly used

1138
00:54:04,130 --> 00:54:07,210
to treat blood pressure
and heart diseases

1139
00:54:07,210 --> 00:54:08,520
and things like that.

1140
00:54:08,520 --> 00:54:10,770
So essentially, they found
this very interesting thing

1141
00:54:10,770 --> 00:54:14,890
that people who are taking
these ACE inhibitors,

1142
00:54:14,890 --> 00:54:18,753
they actually have a heightened
risk for Alzheimer's over here.

1143
00:54:18,753 --> 00:54:20,170
So what they wanted
to do was they

1144
00:54:20,170 --> 00:54:23,410
wanted to understand
why is that reason.

1145
00:54:23,410 --> 00:54:26,610
So they did years of
experimental work,

1146
00:54:26,610 --> 00:54:29,610
and they were able to
come up with a mechanism

1147
00:54:29,610 --> 00:54:30,650
and a prediction.

1148
00:54:30,650 --> 00:54:35,730
So it had a very complex
nine-step cascade over here.

1149
00:54:35,730 --> 00:54:37,863
So what we asked the
AI to do was to--

1150
00:54:37,863 --> 00:54:39,030
we gave it the same problem.

1151
00:54:39,030 --> 00:54:41,890
And we asked it, what do you
think is the reason why ACE

1152
00:54:41,890 --> 00:54:46,210
inhibitors lead to increased
risk of Alzheimer's?

1153
00:54:46,210 --> 00:54:49,690
And essentially
what it did was it

1154
00:54:49,690 --> 00:54:53,050
recapitulated their entire
nine-step mechanistic cascade

1155
00:54:53,050 --> 00:54:54,370
over here.

1156
00:54:54,370 --> 00:54:57,170
But the cool bit was
it was not only that.

1157
00:54:57,170 --> 00:54:59,690
It also predicted
one of the key steps

1158
00:54:59,690 --> 00:55:02,250
that the scientists
had actually missed.

1159
00:55:02,250 --> 00:55:04,510
So again, to
simplify this a lot,

1160
00:55:04,510 --> 00:55:10,210
essentially the thing is, so
if you take ACE inhibitors,

1161
00:55:10,210 --> 00:55:14,570
they modulate this chemical
signal called bradykinin.

1162
00:55:14,570 --> 00:55:15,970
And that, in turn, leads to--

1163
00:55:15,970 --> 00:55:19,130

1164
00:55:19,130 --> 00:55:22,490
there's a receptor on the
surface of your brain cells

1165
00:55:22,490 --> 00:55:23,850
called this B2R receptor.

1166
00:55:23,850 --> 00:55:27,510
So it triggers
this B2R receptor.

1167
00:55:27,510 --> 00:55:31,650
And that, in turn, leads to
neurodegeneration over here.

1168
00:55:31,650 --> 00:55:35,210
So essentially, what the AI
said was that the missing link

1169
00:55:35,210 --> 00:55:38,210
was essentially this
link between bradykinin

1170
00:55:38,210 --> 00:55:40,330
and this B2R receptor.

1171
00:55:40,330 --> 00:55:42,730
So what these
researchers then did was

1172
00:55:42,730 --> 00:55:44,370
they went back to
the lab, and they

1173
00:55:44,370 --> 00:55:47,010
did this is a protein
stability assay, which is

1174
00:55:47,010 --> 00:55:48,790
called a chase assay over here.

1175
00:55:48,790 --> 00:55:52,310
And essentially, that missing
step was also, again, validated.

1176
00:55:52,310 --> 00:55:54,930
So AI was not only
able to recapitulate

1177
00:55:54,930 --> 00:55:56,770
what they had exactly
done, but it was also

1178
00:55:56,770 --> 00:55:58,930
able to fill in the
finer details over here

1179
00:55:58,930 --> 00:56:01,010
and complete their hypotheses.

1180
00:56:01,010 --> 00:56:04,250
And this is actually
a good example

1181
00:56:04,250 --> 00:56:08,730
of how this agentic scaffold is
actually better than baselines,

1182
00:56:08,730 --> 00:56:12,170
because what we did was we also
gave the same problem to Claude

1183
00:56:12,170 --> 00:56:13,930
and GPT-5.

1184
00:56:13,930 --> 00:56:15,690
And essentially, while
both those systems

1185
00:56:15,690 --> 00:56:19,130
were able to get this first step
and the high-level hypotheses

1186
00:56:19,130 --> 00:56:22,130
right, they were actually not
able to get the full details

1187
00:56:22,130 --> 00:56:23,450
over here.

1188
00:56:23,450 --> 00:56:26,330
So you really needed
this agentic harness

1189
00:56:26,330 --> 00:56:29,850
to be able to arrive at the
key details and the specifics

1190
00:56:29,850 --> 00:56:31,010
over here.

1191
00:56:31,010 --> 00:56:33,690
So that's again-- it's telling
you how this agentic harness is

1192
00:56:33,690 --> 00:56:34,850
actually helpful.

1193
00:56:34,850 --> 00:56:37,290
The time that you're
spending on verification,

1194
00:56:37,290 --> 00:56:39,650
coming up with the right
kind of hypotheses,

1195
00:56:39,650 --> 00:56:42,930
that is how you get to much
better results and genuinely

1196
00:56:42,930 --> 00:56:47,730
novel scientific
insights and discoveries.

1197
00:56:47,730 --> 00:56:51,870
And yeah, the last one I want to
very quickly highlight-- again,

1198
00:56:51,870 --> 00:56:54,250
one of the things that
I do, besides trying

1199
00:56:54,250 --> 00:56:56,810
to help improve the
system, is actually just

1200
00:56:56,810 --> 00:56:59,170
ask it interesting questions.

1201
00:56:59,170 --> 00:57:02,210
So one of the things that
I've been interested in

1202
00:57:02,210 --> 00:57:05,850
is this relationship between
neurodegenerative diseases

1203
00:57:05,850 --> 00:57:07,170
and cancer.

1204
00:57:07,170 --> 00:57:11,910
It's actually well known that if
you are an Alzheimer's patient,

1205
00:57:11,910 --> 00:57:14,930
you actually have much
lower risk of cancer.

1206
00:57:14,930 --> 00:57:18,250
So that's not
super well studied.

1207
00:57:18,250 --> 00:57:21,490
So what I asked the system
was to just go and read up

1208
00:57:21,490 --> 00:57:24,170
the literature and
then tell me, what

1209
00:57:24,170 --> 00:57:27,810
are maybe some
understudied pathways

1210
00:57:27,810 --> 00:57:31,530
that are very common in neuro
diseases, like neuro genes,

1211
00:57:31,530 --> 00:57:35,970
essentially, that might actually
be implicated in cancer as well?

1212
00:57:35,970 --> 00:57:39,330
So just tell me, these genes and
these specific forms of cancer

1213
00:57:39,330 --> 00:57:41,810
that we should go and look at.

1214
00:57:41,810 --> 00:57:43,010
So when I gave this prompt--

1215
00:57:43,010 --> 00:57:44,843
and this is the inverse
comorbidity problem.

1216
00:57:44,843 --> 00:57:46,490
So you have one
disease, and that

1217
00:57:46,490 --> 00:57:48,370
just reduces the
chance of you having

1218
00:57:48,370 --> 00:57:50,012
another disease over here.

1219
00:57:50,012 --> 00:57:51,970
And essentially, if you
were to identify enough

1220
00:57:51,970 --> 00:57:53,490
of these pathways,
they could also

1221
00:57:53,490 --> 00:57:59,650
be targets to build drugs for
those specific indications.

1222
00:57:59,650 --> 00:58:01,730
So I gave the
system this prompt.

1223
00:58:01,730 --> 00:58:07,150
And it came up with a
pair of genes over here,

1224
00:58:07,150 --> 00:58:10,250
which are common neuro genes.

1225
00:58:10,250 --> 00:58:15,010
So there was these two
genes called DHX9 and SRRM4.

1226
00:58:15,010 --> 00:58:17,810
And not only that,
it also told me,

1227
00:58:17,810 --> 00:58:20,010
if you want to
validate this gene,

1228
00:58:20,010 --> 00:58:22,210
you should reach out
to this professor

1229
00:58:22,210 --> 00:58:25,850
Dr. Filippo Bellagio
based in Germany.

1230
00:58:25,850 --> 00:58:27,470
